WEBVTT

00:00.473 --> 00:02.424
- [Voiceover] Let's
now introduce ourselves

00:02.424 --> 00:05.656
to the idea of a differential equation.

00:05.656 --> 00:06.721
And as we'll see, differential equations

00:07.306 --> 00:10.688
are super useful for
modeling and simulating

00:10.688 --> 00:13.573
phenomena and understanding
how they operate.

00:13.589 --> 00:15.038
But we'll get into that later.

00:15.038 --> 00:17.772
For now let's just think
about or at least look at

00:17.772 --> 00:20.523
what a differential equation actually is.

00:21.572 --> 00:24.938
So if I were to write, so
let's see here is an example

00:24.938 --> 00:27.139
of differential equation,
if I were to write that

00:27.139 --> 00:31.224
the second derivative of y plus two times

00:31.224 --> 00:36.007
the first derivative of y
is equal to three times y,

00:36.007 --> 00:38.189
this right over here is
a differential equation.

00:38.189 --> 00:39.722
Another way we could write it if we said

00:39.722 --> 00:42.305
that y is a function of
x, we could write this

00:42.305 --> 00:43.907
in function notation.

00:43.907 --> 00:45.855
We could write the second
derivative of our function

00:45.855 --> 00:49.920
with respect to x plus two
times the first derivative

00:50.505 --> 00:54.108
of our function is equal to
three times our function.

00:54.788 --> 00:58.240
Or if we wanted to use
the Leibniz notation,

00:58.024 --> 01:03.024
we could also write, the
second derivative of y

01:03.987 --> 01:08.938
with respect to x plus two
times the first derivative

01:08.938 --> 01:13.422
of y with respect to x is
equal to three times y.

01:14.037 --> 01:17.755
All three of these equations
are really representing

01:17.755 --> 01:20.856
the same thing, they're saying
OK, can I find functions

01:20.856 --> 01:23.023
where the second
derivative of the function

01:23.023 --> 01:25.025
plus two times the first
derivative of the function

01:25.223 --> 01:27.937
is equal to three times
the function itself.

01:28.355 --> 01:31.339
So just to be clear,
these are all essentially

01:31.339 --> 01:33.655
saying the same thing.

01:33.806 --> 01:36.089
And you might have just
caught from how I described it

01:36.089 --> 01:38.092
that the solution to a
differential equation

01:38.389 --> 01:41.238
is a function, or a class of functions.

01:41.323 --> 01:44.322
It's not just a value or a set of values.

01:44.354 --> 01:47.673
So the solution here, so the solution

01:47.673 --> 01:52.673
to a differential equation is a function,

01:53.738 --> 01:56.672
or a set of functions,
or a class of functions.

01:56.756 --> 01:58.805
It's important to contrast this relative

01:58.805 --> 02:00.027
to a traditional equation.

02:00.027 --> 02:01.421
So let me write that down.

02:01.474 --> 02:04.549
So a traditional equation,
maybe I shouldn't say

02:05.224 --> 02:06.687
traditional equation,
differential equations

02:06.687 --> 02:08.306
have been around for a while.

02:08.589 --> 02:11.787
So let me write this as
maybe an algebraic equation

02:11.787 --> 02:13.355
that you're familiar with.

02:13.622 --> 02:18.622
An algebraic equation
might look something like,

02:19.389 --> 02:21.623
and I'll just write up a simple quadratic.

02:21.856 --> 02:26.856
Say x squared plus three x
plus two is equal to zero.

02:30.139 --> 02:32.673
The solutions to this algebraic equation

02:32.673 --> 02:35.255
are going to be numbers,
or a set of numbers.

02:35.255 --> 02:38.271
We can solve this, it's
going to be x plus two

02:38.271 --> 02:41.639
times x plus one is equal to zero.

02:41.924 --> 02:45.506
So x could be equal to
negative two or x could

02:45.506 --> 02:47.105
be equal to negative one.

02:47.171 --> 02:52.171
The solutions here are
numbers, or a set of values

02:53.003 --> 02:55.225
that satisfy the equation.

02:55.522 --> 02:58.390
Here it's a relationship
between a function

02:58.039 --> 02:59.286
and its derivatives.

02:59.637 --> 03:01.655
And so the solutions, or the solution,

03:01.655 --> 03:03.637
is going to be a function
or a set of functions.

03:03.637 --> 03:05.456
Now let's make that a
little more tangible.

03:05.456 --> 03:08.439
What would a solution to
something like any of these three,

03:08.439 --> 03:10.072
which really represent the same thing,

03:10.072 --> 03:12.355
what would a solution actually look like?

03:12.355 --> 03:16.623
Actually let me move
this over a little bit.

03:17.121 --> 03:19.170
Move this over a little bit.

03:19.307 --> 03:21.508
So we can take a look at what some

03:21.508 --> 03:22.601
of these solutions could look like.

03:23.438 --> 03:24.722
Let me erase this a little.

03:25.038 --> 03:27.022
This little stuff that
I have right over here.

03:27.637 --> 03:31.071
So I'm just gonna give you
examples of solutions here.

03:31.071 --> 03:34.539
We'll verify that these
indeed are solutions

03:34.539 --> 03:36.888
for I guess this is really
just one differential equation

03:36.888 --> 03:38.190
represented in different ways.

03:38.019 --> 03:39.835
But you'll hopefully
appreciate what a solution

03:40.006 --> 03:42.304
to a differential equation looks like.

03:42.389 --> 03:45.272
And that there is often
more than one solution.

03:45.272 --> 03:49.087
There's a whole class of functions
that could be a solution.

03:49.689 --> 03:52.556
So one solution to this
differential equation,

03:52.556 --> 03:54.224
and I'll just write it as our first one.

03:54.605 --> 03:57.339
So one solution, I'll call it y one.

03:57.339 --> 03:59.523
And I could even write it as y one of x

03:59.523 --> 04:01.548
to make it explicit that
it is a function of x.

04:02.005 --> 04:05.364
One solutions is y one of x is equal to e

04:05.364 --> 04:08.022
to the negative three x.

04:08.022 --> 04:10.423
And I encourage you to
pause this video right now

04:10.423 --> 04:13.355
and find the first derivative of y one,

04:13.355 --> 04:16.437
and the second derivative of
y one, and verify that it does

04:16.437 --> 04:19.288
indeed satisfy this differential equation.

04:20.137 --> 04:22.455
So I'm assuming you've had a go at it.

04:22.588 --> 04:23.671
So let's work through this together.

04:23.671 --> 04:25.473
So that's y one.

04:25.622 --> 04:29.555
So the first derivative of y one,

04:29.555 --> 04:31.289
so we just have to do the chain rule here,

04:31.289 --> 04:33.706
the derivative of negative
three x with respect to x

04:33.706 --> 04:35.024
is just negative three.

04:35.256 --> 04:36.972
And the derivative of e
to the negative three x

04:36.972 --> 04:39.975
with respect to negative three x is just e

04:39.975 --> 04:41.922
to the negative three x.

04:42.256 --> 04:46.273
And if we take the second
derivative of y one,

04:46.273 --> 04:48.368
this is equal to the same exact idea,

04:49.223 --> 04:50.688
the derivative of this is
three times negative three

04:50.688 --> 04:55.273
is going to be nine e
to the negative three x.

04:55.756 --> 04:56.762
And now we could just
substitute these values

04:57.356 --> 04:59.223
into the differential
equation, or these expressions

04:59.223 --> 05:01.007
into the differential
equation to verify that this

05:01.007 --> 05:05.089
is indeed going to be
true for this function.

05:05.089 --> 05:06.372
So let's verify that.

05:06.605 --> 05:11.605
So we first have the
second derivative of y.

05:12.856 --> 05:14.723
So that's that term right over there.

05:14.837 --> 05:19.789
So we have nine e to the negative three x

05:19.789 --> 05:24.789
plus two times the first derivative.

05:26.187 --> 05:29.888
So that's going to be two
times this right over here.

05:30.005 --> 05:33.507
So it's going to be minus
six, I'll just write

05:33.507 --> 05:37.654
plus negative six e to
the negative three x.

05:37.654 --> 05:40.256
Notice I just took this two
times the first derivative.

05:40.256 --> 05:44.805
Two times the first derivative
is going to be equal to,

05:44.805 --> 05:48.203
or needs to be equal to, if
this indeed does satisfy,

05:48.203 --> 05:52.340
if y one does indeed satisfy
the differential equation,

05:52.034 --> 05:54.682
this needs to be equal to three times y.

05:54.988 --> 05:59.023
Well three times y is three
times e to the negative three x.

05:59.188 --> 06:02.189
Three e to the negative three x.

06:02.606 --> 06:04.305
Let's see if that indeed is true.

06:04.523 --> 06:07.937
So these two terms right over here,

06:07.937 --> 06:09.755
nine e to the negative three x,

06:09.755 --> 06:12.788
essentially minus six e
to the negative three x,

06:12.788 --> 06:15.724
that's gonna be three e
to the negative three x.

06:15.938 --> 06:20.938
Which is indeed equal to three
e to the negative three x.

06:22.923 --> 06:27.923
So y one is indeed a solution
to this differential equation.

06:28.288 --> 06:31.223
But as we'll see, it is
not the only solution

06:31.223 --> 06:32.588
to this differential equation.

06:32.839 --> 06:37.839
For example, let's say y
two is equal to e to the x

06:44.056 --> 06:46.488
is also a solution to this
differential equation.

06:46.488 --> 06:47.973
And I encourage you to
pause the video again

06:47.973 --> 06:49.756
and verify that it's a solution.

06:50.572 --> 06:51.889
So assuming you've had a go at it.

06:52.055 --> 06:54.272
So the first derivative of this
is pretty straight-forward,

06:54.272 --> 06:55.521
is e to the x.

06:55.521 --> 06:58.173
Second derivative, one
of the profound things

06:58.173 --> 07:00.306
of the exponential function,
the second derivative here

07:00.306 --> 07:01.773
is also e to the x.

07:02.224 --> 07:05.623
So the second derivative,
let me just do it

07:05.623 --> 07:06.974
in those same colors.

07:07.022 --> 07:11.423
So the second derivative
is going to be e to the x

07:11.423 --> 07:13.439
plus two times e to the x

07:17.621 --> 07:22.621
is indeed going to be equal
to three times e to the x.

07:23.255 --> 07:24.522
This is absolutely going to be true.

07:24.522 --> 07:27.523
E to the x plus two e to the
x is three e to the x.

07:27.589 --> 07:32.170
So y two is also a solution
to this differential equation.

07:32.622 --> 07:33.855
So that's a start.

07:33.855 --> 07:35.839
In the next few videos,
we'll explore this more.

07:35.839 --> 07:37.706
We'll start to see what
the solutions look like,

07:37.706 --> 07:38.761
what classes of solutions are,

07:39.256 --> 07:40.474
techniques for solving them,

07:40.474 --> 07:43.140
visualizing solutions to
